name: observation
kind: inferred
shape: categorical
face: in
membrane: false
note: |
  A property of the system was noticed and confirmed, but nothing previously
  believed was flipped by it. Use when a probe surfaces real behaviour no prior
  belief spoke to — an undocumented coupling, an off-axis quirk found while
  chasing something else, a mechanism observed but not yet explained.

  Distinct from `contradiction`, which requires a prior belief to overturn, and
  from `addition`, which reports work added on purpose. An observation is
  incidental: it fell out of doing something else and would be lost if nobody
  wrote it down.

  Deliberately `membrane: false`. An observation is not yet a belief — no
  rationale attached, nothing repudiated. It earns a fragment only once someone
  works out WHY, and it is usually better served by a pin that makes the
  behaviour fail loudly. Prefer evolving it out into the suite.
route: surface
